# wraith — clean up a Rust package

DESTRUCTIVE: maybe

About to finalize a PR or hand off a Rust package. Want a quick checklist of what's stale, unused, over-complex, or AI-slop-flavored.

app :wraith__clean_up_a_rust_package do
  title       "wraith — clean up a Rust package"
  description "DESTRUCTIVE: maybe About to finalize a PR or hand off a Rust package."
end

## Workflow

Run from the package root (or workspace root).

### 1. dead code — pub items nothing references
```bash
  wraith dead-code
```

### 2. unused dependencies in Cargo.toml
```bash
  wraith unused-deps
```

### 3. over-complex functions (cyclo>15 or cog>25)
```bash
  wraith health
```

### 4. AI-slop signatures (glob imports, lint-escapes, placeholder prose)
```bash
  wraith slop
```

### 5. near-duplicate fn bodies
```bash
  wraith dupes
```

## The shortcut: `wraith audit`

Runs dead-code + unused-deps but ONLY on files changed in your current git branch. Good for a fast "what did this PR introduce" check.

```bash
  wraith audit
```

## Auto-fix (with care)

`wraith fix` can remove dead pub items + unused deps. Dry-run by default — preview the diff first.

### preview what fix would change
```bash
  wraith fix --dry-run
```

### actually apply (after reviewing diff)
```bash
  wraith fix --write
  # then: cargo build && cargo test
```

## Gotchas

- `dead-code` flags PUB items with no in-workspace refs — does
NOT flag pub items consumed by /downstream/ crates. If your package is a library, dead-code in the public surface may be legitimate API.
- `health` thresholds (cyclo/cog) are conservative defaults;
override in `.wraithrc.json` if your codebase has a higher natural floor.
- `slop` flags `use super::*` which is idiomatic in `#[cfg(test)]`
modules — ignore those hits.

